## 2. Inherited Architecture and Outstanding Problems

The architecture of v0.3 may be summarised as a three-layer structure with explicit logical dependencies.

**Metaphysical Layer (L-I):** The universe is conceived as a participatory information-processing system in which observation is constitutive rather than incidental to physical reality. Possibility \( \mathcal{W} \) — the modal state space of coherent configurations accessible from the current state, weighted by thermodynamic plausibility — is the root ontological node. Consciousness is defined as the physical mechanism by which that space is navigated, evaluated, and expanded: a process through which a physical substrate achieves (i) phenomenal experience, (ii) self-modelling, and (iii) the capacity to generate and evaluate possible futures. This definition draws on neurophenomenology (Varela 1996) and integrated information theory (Tononi 2008) while remaining substrate-agnostic.

**Axiological Layer (L-II):** Since all deliberating agents necessarily act under uncertainty, and rational deliberation requires a non-empty accessible future state space, the permanent foreclosure of that space is pre-axiologically prohibited. This meta-axiom is derived from decision theory (Savage 1954) rather than asserted as an intuition: expected utility calculation is formally undefined when the state space collapses to a single outcome, rendering deliberation impossible. Narrative Integration was introduced as the Seventh Branch: the process through which conscious agents construct, maintain, and transmit stories locating their existence within a larger meaningful arc, binding local experience to the civilisation-scale project of expanding possible futures.

**Governance Layer (L-III):** A formalised Fractal Principle operationalises the informal claim that healthy structures at one scale should reinforce health at all scales. Seven operational branches (Knowledge, Cooperation, Creation, Exploration, Preservation, Transformation, Narrative Integration) derive from the foundational structure. A lexicographic ethics of scale with explicit exceptions governs trade-offs across levels. A Scale-Conflict Matrix with four weighted variables (irreversibility, cross-scale externality, recoverability time horizon, epistemic confidence) formalises intervention thresholds. A conditional Multiplanetary Imperative states that expansion is ethically required if and only if it satisfies recursive stewardship across all three layers.

Three challenges remain, as identified in Section 1. We address them in turn, demonstrating that each resolution strengthens rather than compromises the framework's coherence.

