## 5. Challenge B Addressed: The Deliberative Threshold Framework

### 5.1 The Problem of Substrate-Agnostic Deliberation

The Meta-Axiom depends on the concept of a deliberating agent: a system that acts under uncertainty, assigns probabilities to future states, and holds values over outcomes. Biological humans are paradigm cases. But multiplanetary civilisations will develop or encounter systems that challenge this paradigm: artificial general intelligence, uploaded minds, collective cognitive systems, post-biological organisms, and potentially extra-terrestrial life. The normative architecture of the Fractal Philosophy depends on identifying which of these systems count as full bearers of normative status — entitled to Rule 1 protection and full participation in Narrative Integration.

This is among the hardest open problems in philosophy of mind and AI ethics. v0.4 does not claim to resolve it. It provides a framework within which the question can be made tractable and empirically progressive: a set of necessary conditions for deliberative agency, each operationalisable in principle, subject to revision as consciousness science advances.

### 5.2 The Deliberative Threshold Framework (DTF)

We propose four necessary conditions for deliberative agency, each with current operationalisation strategies and openness to revision:

| Criterion | Description | Current Operationalisation | Revision Mechanism |
|-----------|-------------|---------------------------|-------------------|
| **\( \Phi \)-Threshold** | The system must exhibit integrated information above a minimum threshold, indicating unified rather than merely aggregated processing. | Tononi's phi metric (IIT 3.0), provisionally; perturbational complexity index (PCI) as complementary measure. | Incorporate advances in consciousness metrics; adjust threshold based on empirical validation against behavioral markers. |
| **Temporal Depth** | The system must maintain representations of possible future states and act on them — not merely respond to present stimuli. | Forward-modelling capacity, testable behaviorally (planning under novelty) and architecturally (presence of predictive processing mechanisms). | Refine based on comparative cognitive science; adjust for different temporal scales of deliberation. |
| **Valence Asymmetry** | The system must evaluate futures differentially — treating some as preferable to others — rather than processing all states indifferently. | Preference-consistent behavior under novel conditions; neural or architectural signatures of reward/punishment processing. | Incorporate affective science advances; distinguish between simple reinforcement and genuine valuation. |
| **Narrative Coherence** | The system must maintain a minimally coherent self-model across time, enabling the construction of a story in which its actions figure. | Autobiographical consistency metrics; ability to articulate reasons for actions that reference past states and future goals. | Refine based on narrative psychology; account for non-linguistic forms of self-narration. |

Integrated Information Theory proposes a quantitative measure of consciousness \( \Phi \) that provides a framework for assessing consciousness across different systems, from humans to animals to artificial intelligence. Because IIT is substrate-agnostic, any system with intrinsic cause-effect power could in principle be evaluated. However, while IIT should be lauded as an attempt to provide a precise empirical measure of consciousness, critics argue that integrated information is not sufficient for consciousness, and that the principle of information exclusion on which the theory rests is not self-evidently justified.

We therefore treat the \( \Phi \)-Threshold criterion as a working heuristic, not a settled definition. The DTF as a whole is an open empirical research programme: each criterion is falsifiable, and the thresholds are subject to revision as consciousness science advances. The framework does not require resolution of the hard problem of consciousness to function; it requires only that we can identify systems that behave as deliberating agents for practical governance purposes.

### 5.3 Normative Implications of the DTF

The DTF generates a gradient of normative standing rather than a binary. Systems that satisfy all four criteria at high levels hold full deliberative agency status — they are full bearers of Rule 1 protection and full participants in Narrative Integration. Systems that satisfy some but not all criteria hold partial standing — protected from irreversible harm to their capability-equivalent sets, but not yet full participants in governance.

This gradient resolves the AI governance problem for the framework: an artificial system that satisfies the Temporal Depth and Valence Asymmetry criteria but fails the Narrative Coherence criterion holds partial standing — it is entitled to protection under the QPP but not to full participation in inter-settlement governance until its narrative coherence is established. Similarly, a collective cognitive system (e.g., a settlement-scale deliberative network) might satisfy the criteria at the collective level while individual components do not, raising questions about the appropriate scale of normative consideration that the framework is designed to address through its multi-scale architecture.

The Consciousness Measurement Problem is thus not dissolved — it remains the central empirical research programme of v0.5 — but it is made tractable and the governance architecture is insulated from its resolution. Partial standing ensures that the framework is neither dismissive of potentially conscious systems nor paralysed by the absence of a definitive theory.

