## 7. The Fractal Principle: Revised Formalisation

The Fractal Principle of v0.3 is revised to incorporate the Possibility Function explicitly, providing a common metric by which health is measured across all scales.

**Revised Fractal Principle:** A governance structure is fractally coherent if and only if actions taken at level \( L_i \) that increase \( \mathcal{P}_f \) at \( L_i \) do not decrease \( \mathcal{P}_f \) at any other level \( L_j \), after accounting for communication delays \( \tau_{ij} \) and emergent dynamics.

Formally: let \( \Phi(a, L_i) \) denote the effect of action \( a \) at level \( L_i \) on the Possibility Function \( \mathcal{P}_f(L_j) \) for all \( j \neq i \). Fractal integrity holds when:

$$\text{sign}\left(\nabla_a \mathcal{P}_f(L_i)\right) = \text{sign}\left(\nabla_a \mathcal{P}_f(L_j)\right) \quad \forall i \neq j$$

after delay correction. This is the formal operationalisation of the informal principle that a healthy structure at one scale should reinforce health at all scales. The Possibility Function provides the common metric by which health is measured across all scales, resolving the prior version's implicit reliance on congruent intuitions across levels.

We operationalise this using graph-theoretic stability. Let the inter-scale interaction graph have adjacency matrix \( A \) with modularity \( Q \) and algebraic connectivity \( \lambda_2 \). Fractal integrity is maintained when \( \lambda_2 > \tau_{\text{crit}} \) and \( Q \in [Q_{\min}, Q_{\max}] \), preventing both global fragility (over-centralisation) and catastrophic fragmentation (isolation). Under these conditions, \( \Phi \) exhibits Lyapunov stability: perturbations at any scale dampen recursively rather than cascade destructively.

