## 10. The Multiplanetary Imperative (v0.4 Revision)

We reject expansion as an unconditional duty. Instead:

**The Multiplanetary Imperative (v0.4):** A conscious lineage ought to expand to multiple worlds if and only if:

(i) Expansion predictably increases \( \mathcal{P}_f \) across all scales (\( \nabla_e \mathcal{P}_f > 0 \) globally);

(ii) No individual variable (\( D \), \( C \), \( R \), \( M \)) is reduced below its minimum viable threshold at any scale;

(iii) The target environment is governed under the QPP — specifically, that worlds with extant deliberating agents or life capable of developing deliberative agency are protected under strict non-interference protocols;

(iv) The Narrative Integration branch has produced inter-generational meaning structures sufficient to sustain voluntary commitment across the full time horizon of the expansion project.

Condition (i) replaces the v0.3 formulation's more diffuse language about "increasing total possible futures" with a formally tractable test. Conditions (ii)–(iv) provide the constraint set. The imperative is conditional: expansion without \( \mathcal{P}_f \)-increase is not ethically neutral — it is actively prohibited under Rule 1, because it expends irreversible civilisational resources without expanding \( \mathcal{W}^* \).

This formulation directly addresses decolonial critiques of space expansion rhetoric. Historical colonialism operated through scale parasitism: the metropole extracted resources from the periphery without reciprocity, permanently degrading local nodes. This is inherently anti-fractal, as it violates recursive stability and accelerates fractal dissolution. The Fractal Philosophy mandates planet-based solidarity: autonomous self-determination, reciprocal resource and knowledge exchange, and the prohibition of permanent external dependency structures. Expansion, under this framework, is not conquest but networked node proliferation.

