## 13. Experimental Testability: The \( \mathcal{P}_f \)-Instrumented Multi-Agent Simulation

Normative frameworks require stress-testing. We propose a heterogeneous multi-agent simulation instrumented with the Possibility Function as the primary outcome metric.

**Design:** A multi-agent simulation (Mesa/Python or NetLogo) representing Earth, Mars, and the Asteroid Belt as distinct settlement nodes. Agents are heterogeneous, parameterised by Five-Factor Model personality traits, deliberative agency scores (DTF criteria), and role diversity. Communication is modelled as asynchronous message queues with variable delay parameters, forcing local clusters to rely on immediate reciprocity rather than global enforcement — validating the structural necessity of Level 1 Conflict Resolution.

**\( \mathcal{P}_f \) instrumentation:** At each simulation timestep, the four variables \( D \), \( C \), \( R \), \( M \) are computed at the node level and globally using operational definitions specified in Section 6.2. The primary outcome is whether \( \mathcal{P}_f \) across all nodes exceeds \( \mathcal{P}_{min} \) across a 1,000-year simulation horizon under resource shocks, governance fragmentation, and communication blackouts.

**DTF test:** A secondary test introduces artificial agents with partial DTF scores at simulation Year 200. Settlement nodes must make governance decisions about their status. The simulation tests whether DTF Drift (Failure Mode 7) emerges under various incentive structures and whether the independent DTF review mechanism prevents it.

**Historical calibration:** The simulation is benchmarked against two contrasting historical cases: (a) the Montreal Protocol ozone regime, a successful multi-party coordination under uncertainty with strong \( R \) and \( M \) outcomes; and (b) the fragmentation of Reconstruction-era governance in the United States post-1877, a case of high initial \( \mathcal{P}_f \) followed by politically driven DTF Drift that contracted \( \mathcal{W}^* \) for generations. These cases provide empirical \( \mathcal{P}_f \) trajectory shapes against which to validate simulation dynamics.

**Validation criteria:** The simulation is considered successful if: (i) fractal ethics outperform utilitarian longtermism and realpolitik in high-delay, high-shock scenarios on the primary outcome metric; (ii) the Scale-Conflict Matrix thresholds produce intervention decisions that align with expert normative judgment in test cases; and (iii) the DTF review mechanism prevents normative corruption under political pressure in the artificial agent test.

