## 15. The Core Theorem and Conclusion

### 15.1 The Core Theorem

The doctoral review proposed that the deepest idea of the project might eventually be compressed into a single sentence. We propose that sentence as the Core Theorem of the Fractal Philosophy:

> **A civilisation flourishes in proportion to its capacity to expand meaningful possibilities across scales without destroying the conditions that make future possibility possible.**

This sentence is not merely a slogan. It is formally tractable: "flourishes" maps to \( \mathcal{P}_f > \mathcal{P}_{min} \); "expand meaningful possibilities" maps to \( \nabla \mathcal{P}_f > 0 \) over the Qualified Possibility Space \( \mathcal{W}^* \); "across scales" maps to the Fractal Principle's invariance condition; "without destroying the conditions" maps to Rule 1 of the lexicographic ethics and the QPP constraint; "that make future possibility possible" maps to the Meta-Axiom's pre-axiological prohibition on foreclosing the conditions of deliberation.

The entire three-layer architecture — metaphysical, axiological, and governance — is thus compressed into a single sentence that is both scientifically legible and humanly motivating. This is the criterion by which the framework can attract the billions of voluntary participants that Narrative Integration requires.

